== Study Limitations
This study has several limitations regarding the design of the study and the number of participants which should be acknowledged. To begin with, this study has applied only one case study, (Brain Stimulation), when investigating relevant therapist interfaces for rehabilitation through exergames. Brain Stimulation is a company who mainly focus on stroke-patients or patients with neurological conditions and mental fatigue through XR-based exergames, and while this provided in-depth understanding of one specific rehabilitation context, we believe that other relevant systems in other fields might differ significantly in terms of system interactions and processes, field, therapist needs and clinical workflows. Not accounting for other existing systems might therefore have created assumptions and insights tied to a single context, ultimately affecting the design of the co-design session including its dimensions and overall results and direction of this study. We however attempted to reduce this bias by reviewing research on existing automated systems within rehabilitation and therapists interfaces used for exergame-based rehabilitation. 

In regards to the co-design session, we believe that introducing one scenario after another to the participants might have affected how the later scenarios were perceived. Although the intention of doing so was to progressively expose the participants to the level of automation within the four scenarios, we are unsure if introducing the scenarios in reverse order from _S4-S1_ would have affected their perception and attitude towards the same scenarios. Additionally, while conducting the co-design sessions individually with the participants gave them enough time to think and elaborate their thoughts and opinions, we suppose that the results could be looking differently in group co-design sessions as participants could build upon each others interpretations. More collaborative discussion with diverse expertise could perhaps uncover perspectives that might have been overlooked in our individual co-design sessions. It could potentially even give insights into the conditions necessary for collaboration among colleagues within the system which was not discussed in the individual co-design session. Although there are potential benefits of conducting group co-design session, we believe that even those have their limitations such as biasness or group pressure from dominant individuals. This may lead to a limited range of perspectives rather than a comprehensive representation of all participants' views. We therefore believe that conducting individual co-design sessions was a strength as they allowed us to explore five different perspectives in-depth. 

While the results of this study are of qualitative nature, five participants are still too few to make any definitive and broad conclusions on which dimensions affect therapists sense of agency the most, or how the required agency distribution between human-machine should be through all four therapy phases. However, we still believe that the results of this study are highly reliable and qualitative as our participants had extensive experience with an average of *X years*. That said, the imbalance of 1 physiotherapist and 4 occupational therapists might have affected the understanding of the workflow and necessities for the agency distribution and needs for an AI-supported computational system for exergame-based rehabilitation across different therapeutic fields. 

